Spring boot Security
====================

=> It is spring extension module that provides multiple readymade filters to enable security on spring boot 
   MVC and spring boot rest applications.

=> if the security module is used in spring style then it is called spring security

=> if the security module is used in spring boot style then it is called spring boot security

 ---------------------------------------------------------
| Checking the access permission called authorization      |

| Checking the identity of a user is called authentication |
 ---------------------------------------------------------

 One for Authentication → who are you? (InMemory, DB, LDAP, etc.)

 One for Authorization → what can you access? (roles, permissions, URLs, etc.)


Problem:
------------------------------------------------
[ browser window ]

                  webserver / Application server
+------------------------------------------------------+
|  Servlet container        |   IOC container          |
|                           |                          |
|   [ Servlet Filter comp ] |   [ Java class acting    |
|                           |     as spring bean ]     |
|                           |                          |
+------------------------------------------------------+

=>Here spring bean injection to ServletFilter comp is NOT possible,
  because ServletFilter comp is not taken as spring bean.
  It is managed by ServletContainer, not IOC container.

=> In order to inject dependent Spring bean into servlet filter comp,
   we need to take ServletFilter comp also as Spring bean
   in IOC container.



Solution:
------------------------------------------------
[ browser window ]
         |
         | (1) request
         v

               web server / application server
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
|  ServletContainer                  |  IOC container          |
|                                    |                         |
|   [ DelegatingFilterProxy          |   [ ServletFilter comp  |
|     (ready ServletFilter) ]        |     (as spring bean) ]  |
|   - url pattern: /                  |   - bean id: f1        |
|   - filter name: f1                 |   (Target spring bean) |
|                                    |                         |
|   (2) -> (3) -> (4)                 |   [ Java class         |
|                                    |     as spring bean ]    |
|                                    |   (Dependent bean)      |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+

This time Dependency Injection is possible!


=>if we take servlet filter comp as spring bean in IOC container then any another dependent spring bean object can be injected to that
Servlet filter spring bean .. but that servlet filter can not trap and take requests given browser window .. For this we need to cfg special
ready made ServletFilter comp given by spring security module that is "DelegatingFilterProxy" having url pattern "/" and logical name
matching with ServletFilter comp's bean id.belonging to IOC container


w.r.t diagram
=============
(1) browser gives the request to web application
(2) DelegatingFilterProxy traps and takes the request
(Searches for that ServletFilter comp whose
(3)DelegatingFilterProxy delegates/passes the request to ServletFilter comp that is acting as spring bean in IOC container
bean id matches with DelegatingFilterProxy's logical name)
(4) the logics in ServletFilter comp executes
(5) ServletFilter comp uses the injected dependent spring bean services through method calls.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

=>Security in web application is nothing but enabling Authentication + Authorization on the web application

Authentication :: checking the identity of user using usernames, passwords, thumb impressions, iris , digital signatures and etc ..
Authorization :: Checking the access permissions of the autheticated users on different resources of the Project.
                           (Here the roles of the user will be verified before allowing the user to access different resources of the Project)

       =>Roles are nothing but designations given to users .. based on roles of the users the access permissions on different resources
         will be decided.

       =>All customers, employees of the bank must be authenticated to use bankApp ..
       =>The users having customer role will get less access peromission on the resoruces where as
       the users having employee role will get more access peromission on the resoruces.

   => It is always recomanded to enable authorization of accessing the resoucers not based on the username .. It is recomanded to
      perform on the roles of the users.
   =>during the authentication process.. get the roles of the user and use those roles for authentization.

End User
   |
   v
+-------------------+
|   Authentication  |
| (username, pwd,   |                                                 realm means small DB s/w or repository where the usernames, passwords and roles are 
|  verification)    |                                                 managed.
+-------------------+
   | success -> get roles from realm
   | failure -> 401 error (if authentication fails)
   v
+-------------------+
|   Authorization   |
| (access based on  |
|    roles)         |
+-------------------+
   | success -> access resources
   | failure -> 403 error (if authorization fails)
   |
   +----> Resource1
   +----> Resource2
   +----> Resource3


=====================================
The comps of Security implementation
=====================================
a) Authentication provider / Authentication Info provider
b) Authentication and Authorization manager


a) Authentication provider/a) Authentication Info provider /Securty Realm 

It is the small realm where usernames, passwords ,roles are managed and will be used during both
authentication and authorization. The possible places are

a) Properties file
b) xml file file                             LDAP :: Lightweight Directory Access Protocol
c) json file
d) Db s/w                                    =>In all other Authentication providers we can get the password of the users
e) LDAP server (best)                          if they forgot the passwords .. where in LDAP server .. no proivision to get
g) InMemory DB                                 password .. only resetting of password is possible.
and etc ..


b) Authentication and Authorization manager
                   =>It is the component that verifies given username and password to perform authentication and gives 401 error if
                     authenticatio fails.

                   =>The same component collects the roles of authenticated user and performs Authorization activities accessing different
                     resources .. gives 403 error if authroization fails.

=>The Authentication and Authorization manager can be arranged in two ways

a) Using Programatic approach (bad)
=>Here we need to develop the logics of Authentication and Authorization manager explicitly
  by spending huge amounts of time and brain manually (Not recomanded)

b) Using Declarative approach (good)
=> Max Web server /App servers provide built-in security support by providing built-in
Authentication and Authorization manager ... By adding entries Web.xml file we can activate
that Authentication and Authorization manager (refer security in web application in servlket,jsp env .. )

Limitations of Declaration approch for securiing web applications (working with servlet container
supplied Authentication and Authorization manager through web.xml entries)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
a) Only selected servers support this features .. some times we need to purchase
   License of costly web server or Application server to use this feature
b) As of now this facility possible by adding addtional entries in web.xml file .. i.e not suitable
   in 100% code driven cfgs , spring boot apps (where xml is totally eliminated)
c) When move from one server to another server these configuration in web.xml file may change
   (web.xml entries related to security are not portable across the multiple servers)
d) No support for LDAP server as authentication info provider.
and etc ..

To overcome these problems we use spring security or spring boot security

Advantages of spring security /spring boot security
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
a) Can be used in spring MVC/spring rest /spring micros services apps and also in non-spring
   based web applications like servlet ,jsp web applications, JSF web applications and etc ...
b) The security cfgs code are portable across the multiple servers
c) We can'tifis security irrespective of wheather the underlying server supports the ServletContainer
   level Declarative security service or not (spring security no way related Servlet container's security )
d) Supports different Authentication Info providers includng LDAP.
e) We need not to arrange costly servers only for security ..


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
In spring env or spring boot env .. we can apply security on MVC apps or spring rest apps or micro services Apps
in 3 approaches


a) Using Spring security/spring boot secuirty

i) Basic Authentication (browser generates the dialog box asking username, password)
ii) Form based Authentication ( readymade or user-defined form page will be there asking username, password) (best)
         -> Using InMemory DB as authentication Info provider (RAM Level DB)
         -> Using Properties file as authentication Info provider
         -> Using DB s/w as authentication info provider
                        with the support of spring JDBC/ sprjing ORM/spring data JPA / UserDetails service
         ->Using LDAP server as authentication info provider.

b) Using JWT (JSON web tokens)
(loosing its popularity)

c) Using Oauth 2.x (Open Authorization) / okta                             SSO --- Single Sign On
[ we can use FB , google , twitter login
for SSO  feature]
